Hey! Great project idea. Let me walk you through several approaches to capture Sublime Text's Build Results output into a Rust `String`, starting with the most efficient and working through progressively different methods.

## 1. Direct Process Output Capture (Most Efficient)

This is the most straightforward and efficient approach using `std::process::Command`:

```rust
use std::process::Command;

fn capture_build_output() -> String {
    let output = Command::new("subl")
        .arg("--command")
        .arg("build")
        .output()
        .expect("Failed to execute build command");

    String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stdout).to_string()
}
```

## 2. Streaming with BufReader (Better for Large Output)

More sophisticated - handles large outputs without loading everything into memory at once:

```rust
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader};
use std::process::{Command, Stdio};

fn capture_build_output_streaming() -> String {
    let mut child = Command::new("subl")
        .arg("--command")
        .arg("build")
        .stdout(Stdio::piped())
        .spawn()
        .expect("Failed to start build");

    let stdout = child.stdout.take().expect("Failed to capture stdout");
    let reader = BufReader::new(stdout);

    let mut output = String::new();
    for line in reader.lines() {
        if let Ok(line) = line {
            output.push_str(&line);
            output.push('\n');
        }
    }

    child.wait().expect("Failed to wait for build");
    output
}
```

